SATURATION, which is why the M-tile count is in the table above. The swizzle groups
GROUP_SIZE_M consecutive M-tiles, so once g reaches a rung's own num_pid_m the map
is plain column-major and every larger g is the SAME order:
  T=16: num_pid_m 4, so GROUP_SIZE_M [8, 16, 32, 64] are ONE setting there, not 4
  T=32: num_pid_m 7, so GROUP_SIZE_M [8, 16, 32, 64] are ONE setting there, not 4
  T=64: num_pid_m 10, so GROUP_SIZE_M [16, 32, 64] are ONE setting there, not 3
  T=128: num_pid_m 18, so GROUP_SIZE_M [32, 64] are ONE setting there, not 2
  T=256: num_pid_m 34, so GROUP_SIZE_M [64] are ONE setting there, not 1
  T=384: num_pid_m 50, so GROUP_SIZE_M [64] are ONE setting there, not 1
  T=448: num_pid_m 59, so GROUP_SIZE_M [64] are ONE setting there, not 1
  It bites the single-tile CONTROL rungs hardest, since they hold the fewest
  M-tiles. The control can still separate g=1 from the grouped orders, which is
  what P4 asks of it, but it cannot resolve the grouped ones from each other.
  THE SECOND ARM THAT FIXES IT, and it is worth running both because they fail in
  opposite directions:
    --model qwen2-57b-a14b --tokens 32,64,128,256,512,768,1024
  E=64 and k=8 put num_pid_m near 550 at the top rung, so nothing saturates, and
  the tiles-per-expert ladder is the same 1 to 9. The cost is that one expert's
  up-GEMM weights are 37 MB against mixtral's 235 MB, so on qwen2 L2 may already
  absorb the re-read at GROUP_SIZE_M=1 and leave the swizzle nothing to save. A
  large effect on mixtral and a small one on qwen2 is the mechanism; a large one
  on both, with the control flat, is stronger still.

measured ridge band 160.3-176.2 FLOP/byte; every cell above is below 90% of the low end, so implied_traffic_ratio
is a traffic bound rather than a statement about padded arithmetic.

CONFOUND BOUND: activation re-reads per extra M-tile over weight re-reads per extra
M-tile is exactly BLOCK_M / BLOCK_N = 16/64 = 0.25, so at most 20% of any alpha
fitted here is activation traffic. GROUP_SIZE_M moves that term the OPPOSITE way,
so a falling alpha is a NET traffic statement and not a pure weight one.

